/* ============================================================
   ANSWER KEY - Database Concepts (HARD LEVEL)
   Curriculum:   RetailMart V3
   Note: Day 14 is concept-only, so these are short reference
   answers (no queries to run/verify) matching each question.
   ============================================================ */

/* Q1.  A table Bookings(room_id, day, rate_class, rate) where (room_id, day) -> rate_class -> rate but also rate_class alone determines rate: rate_class -> rate breaks BCNF since rate_class isn't a candidate key. */
/* Q2.  Splitting on a dependency whose determinant isn't a candidate key can separate the two sides so the original dependency can no longer be checked/enforced by a simple constraint across the decomposed tables - it's implied only by re-joining, not stored anywhere. */
/* Q3.  When the BCNF-violating dependency is rare, low-risk, and the decomposition would hurt query simplicity or lose dependency-preservation more than the redundancy actually costs - practical engineering teams often accept 3NF for that tradeoff. */
/* Q4.  A multivalued dependency is when one key determines a set of independent values in another column regardless of a third column's value; Fourth Normal Form (4NF) addresses it by separating independent multivalued facts into their own tables. */
/* Q5.  A table mixing "employee skills" and "employee languages" in one row (with repeated combinations for every skill x language pair) mixes two independent multivalued facts - 4NF splits it into EmployeeSkills and EmployeeLanguages tables. */
/* Q6.  It means deliberately storing redundant or precomputed data (like cached totals or flattened joins) purely to make reads faster; the cost is accepting the risk that redundant copies can drift out of sync and requiring extra logic to keep them consistent. */
/* Q7.  Star-schema fact tables mix measures with foreign keys to denormalized dimensions by design, because dimensional models optimize for fast, simple aggregate queries over huge fact tables, not for eliminating redundancy. */
/* Q8.  OLTP systems are normalized to support fast, safe, concurrent single-row writes with strong integrity; OLAP systems are dimensional/denormalized to support fast large-scale aggregate reads over historical data, where writes are batched, not interactive. */
/* Q9.  Because heavily normalized data requires many JOINs to reassemble a business answer, and JOINs across large tables are expensive - analytical queries favor fewer, wider tables over many small normalized ones. */
/* Q10. A downside is losing the natural key's business meaning and self-descriptiveness for ad-hoc queries/debugging, plus needing to still enforce uniqueness on the natural key separately to avoid duplicate "different id, same real entity" rows. */
/* Q11. A slowly changing dimension means an attribute (like customer tier) changes over time, so "the normalized truth" isn't a single current value - you need a history-aware design (Type 2 SCD) to know what was true at any point in the past. */
/* Q12. It's worth it when the computed value is queried far more often than the base columns change, and recalculating it on every read would be expensive - as long as it's kept in sync (via trigger or recompute) with its source columns. */
/* Q13. A mutual dependency (A -> B and B -> A) means A and B determine each other one-to-one; it implies both A and B are individually candidate keys for the table. */
/* Q14. NULLs make it ambiguous whether "no value" satisfies or breaks a dependency (is NULL equal to another NULL?), so functional-dependency reasoning technically assumes non-null, fully-populated columns to hold cleanly. */
/* Q15. A business rule not expressed as an actual column or constraint (e.g. "a customer's tier caps their max discount") can create a real dependency the schema never enforces, so the data can silently violate a rule the design "looks" fine on paper without knowing about. */
/* Q16. Reading an ER diagram means tracing which entities determine which attributes (functional dependencies) and how keys link tables - understanding the diagram IS understanding the schema's dependencies and referential structure. */
/* Q17. Logical normalization is about how data is organized conceptually into tables/keys to avoid redundancy; physical storage layout (indexes, partitioning, column order) is about how that logical data is actually stored and accessed on disk - both matter because good logical design can still perform poorly without the right physical tuning. */
/* Q18. When the dimension's attributes are small, rarely change, and are always needed alongside every fact row, flattening them in avoids a join the fact table would otherwise always require - common for tiny/static dimensions. */
/* Q19. Sync risk (the denormalized copy drifting from the normalized source after a refresh bug or partial load) and doubled maintenance burden (every schema change must be applied and tested in two places instead of one). */
/* Q20. A materialized view is explicitly understood and labeled as a derived, refreshable copy with a defined recompute path; an OLTP base table duplicating data has no such contract, so nothing guarantees or signals when the two copies diverge. */
/* Q21. A junction table needs its own attributes when a fact belongs to the relationship itself, not to either side - e.g. enrollment_date belongs to the specific (student, course) pairing, not to the student or the course alone, so it can't live anywhere else. */
/* Q22. If the wrong composite key is chosen (e.g. missing a column that should be part of it), non-key columns can end up partially dependent on the true key (2NF violation) while other columns are also transitively dependent on each other (BCNF violation) simultaneously. */
/* Q23. Normalizing avoids storing the same fact multiple times (saving storage and avoiding anomalies), but reconstructing a full record for a query now requires JOINing several tables, which costs more CPU/IO than reading one denormalized row. */
/* Q24. A VIEW recomputes from the live normalized base tables on every query, so it can never drift out of sync, while a materialized base table would need constant manual/triggered updates and risks becoming stale or wrong. */
/* Q25. (1) Normalize the OLTP/transactional core fully for integrity and safe writes. (2) Denormalize only in dedicated read-optimized layers (views, materialized views, marts) that are clearly derived, not source-of-truth. (3) Always weigh the refresh/consistency cost of any denormalization against the read-speed benefit it buys. */

/* Q26. Dirty reads are prevented starting at READ COMMITTED; non-repeatable reads are prevented starting at REPEATABLE READ; phantom reads are fully prevented only at SERIALIZABLE (though Postgres's REPEATABLE READ also blocks them via snapshot isolation). */
/* Q27. READ COMMITTED means each statement sees only data committed before that statement began, preventing dirty reads, but it still permits non-repeatable reads - re-reading the same row later in the same transaction can see a different, newly committed value. */
/* Q28. REPEATABLE READ means a transaction sees a consistent snapshot throughout, so the same row read twice returns the same value, but the SQL standard says it can still permit phantom reads (new rows matching a filter appearing on a re-query). */
/* Q29. SERIALIZABLE guarantees that the result of running transactions concurrently is equivalent to some serial (one-at-a-time) order of those same transactions - no anomalies of any kind. */
/* Q30. It means each individual statement sees a fresh snapshot of committed data as of when that statement starts, so two long reports running concurrently could see slightly different, evolving views of the data across their separate statements, but each statement is internally consistent. */
/* Q31. Write skew is when two transactions each read overlapping data, make decisions based on what they read, and write to different rows in a way that violates a combined constraint neither transaction's own write alone would violate; only SERIALIZABLE prevents it. */
/* Q32. MVCC (Multi-Version Concurrency Control) keeps multiple versions of each row so readers can see a consistent snapshot as of when their transaction/statement started, while writers create new row versions instead of overwriting - so readers never block on writers and vice versa. */
/* Q33. Because MVCC gives the SELECT its own snapshot of the committed data at the time it started, and concurrent writes create new row versions rather than modifying the ones the SELECT is already reading, so the long query keeps seeing its original consistent view throughout. */
/* Q34. A lost update is when two transactions both read the same row, then both write back based on that stale read, and one write silently overwrites the other's change; stronger isolation (REPEATABLE READ+) or explicit locking (SELECT ... FOR UPDATE) prevents it by detecting or blocking the conflicting write. */
/* Q35. T1 reads balance=100, T2 reads balance=100, T1 writes balance-100=... , T2 also writes based on its stale read of 100, overwriting T1's change entirely; the fix is SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (locks the row) or optimistic locking with a version check before the write. */
/* Q36. SELECT ... FOR UPDATE locks the selected rows so no other transaction can modify (or also lock) them until the current transaction commits or rolls back; an analyst-adjacent job might use it to safely reserve/decrement a limited inventory count before finalizing an order. */
/* Q37. T1 locks row A and waits for row B (held by T2); T2 locks row B and waits for row A (held by T1) - neither can proceed; the database's deadlock detector spots the cycle and forcibly rolls back one transaction so the other can continue. */
/* Q38. Stronger isolation requires more locking, waiting, or conflict-checking between concurrent transactions, so fewer transactions can proceed truly in parallel, reducing overall throughput. */
/* Q39. A shared lock allows multiple transactions to read the same row concurrently but blocks writes; an exclusive lock is held by one transaction only and blocks both reads-for-update and writes from anyone else. */
/* Q40. A long-running transaction holds back Postgres's "xmin horizon," preventing VACUUM from cleaning up dead row versions that are technically still needed by that old snapshot, which can cause table/index bloat and slow down maintenance and other queries. */
/* Q41. Because REPEATABLE READ in Postgres uses a single consistent snapshot for the whole transaction (true snapshot isolation), so any row inserted by another transaction after that snapshot was taken simply isn't visible, regardless of how many times you re-query. */
/* Q42. It forces the database to detect and abort more conflicting transactions (serialization failures) as throughput and contention rise, requiring the application to retry those failed transactions, which adds latency and complexity under heavy concurrent load. */
/* Q43. The UNIQUE constraint's underlying index serializes the two inserts internally - whichever transaction's insert is validated/committed first succeeds, and the second one is rejected with a uniqueness violation once it attempts to commit (or immediately, depending on timing). */
/* Q44. Because SERIALIZABLE can abort a transaction (with a serialization_failure) purely due to a detected conflict with another concurrent transaction, even though neither transaction did anything individually wrong - the application must retry, since a plain error would otherwise lose valid work. */
/* Q45. A finance dashboard reading a mid-transaction, not-yet-committed refund or price adjustment (under READ UNCOMMITTED / dirty reads) could show a number that gets rolled back a moment later, reporting money that never actually existed. */
/* Q46. Read-only replicas let analysts run heavy, long queries against a separate copy of the data, so their transactions never hold locks or contend for resources against the OLTP primary that's serving live customer transactions. */
/* Q47. Every change is first written to an append-only write-ahead log (WAL) on durable storage before it's considered committed; if the server crashes, Postgres replays (redoes) the WAL on restart to reconstruct all committed changes that hadn't yet been written to the main data files. */
/* Q48. Durability requires the committed data to actually survive a crash, which means the WAL record must be physically flushed (fsync'd) to durable storage, not just sitting in an OS or disk cache that could be lost on power failure. */
/* Q49. Because only once the WAL record is durably on disk is it guaranteed to survive a crash - acknowledging COMMIT any earlier could tell the client "saved" for data that a crash could still lose. */
/* Q50. Stronger isolation reduces concurrency (more blocking/aborts) and increases developer complexity (handling retries, understanding anomalies); weaker isolation boosts throughput but pushes correctness burden onto explicit locking or careful application logic - there's no free choice, only where you want the cost to land. */

/* Q51. customers(customer_id PK, ...), products(product_id PK, ...), orders(order_id PK, customer_id FK -> customers), order_items(order_item_id PK, order_id FK -> orders, product_id FK -> products, qty) - order_items resolves the many-products-per-order relationship. */
/* Q52. Design promotions(promo_id PK, ...), products(product_id PK, ...), and a junction promotion_products(promo_id FK, product_id FK, discount_percent) with a composite key - the discount lives on the junction table since it's specific to that product-promotion pairing. */
/* Q53. employees(employee_id PK, manager_id FK REFERENCES employees(employee_id), ...) - a self-referencing foreign key where manager_id points to another row's employee_id in the same table. */
/* Q54. store_stock(store_id FK, product_id FK, quantity) with a composite primary key of (store_id, product_id), since the combination uniquely identifies one stock row. */
/* Q55. It's 1:1 (assuming "exactly one" both ways) - model it by making customer_id the primary key of loyalty_membership (and a foreign key to customers), so each customer_id can appear at most once in that table. */
/* Q56. The order side holds the FK - orders(order_id PK, coupon_id FK NULL REFERENCES coupons) - since many orders can reference the same coupon, but each order uses at most one. */
/* Q57. A date dimension dim_date(date_key PK, day, month, year, quarter, day_name, month_name, is_weekend, is_holiday) - useful analytics columns include quarter (for quarterly reporting), day_name (for weekday-pattern analysis), and is_holiday (for excluding/flagging anomalies). */
/* Q58. price_history(price_id PK, product_id FK, price, effective_from, effective_to) with one new row inserted per price change instead of updating a single price column - preserves every historical price with its valid date range. */
/* Q59. ticket_status_history(history_id PK, ticket_id FK REFERENCES tickets, status, changed_at) with one row appended per status change, keyed by a surrogate history_id, so the full status timeline is preserved. */
/* Q60. tags(tag_id PK, tag_name), products(product_id PK, ...), product_tags(product_id FK, tag_id FK) as the junction table with a composite primary key of (product_id, tag_id). */
/* Q61. Polymorphic FKs (one column referencing different tables based on a type flag) break real foreign-key enforcement since the DB can't validate against two possible parent tables; a cleaner alternative is two nullable FK columns (customer_id, store_id) with a CHECK that exactly one is set, or separate addresses_customers / addresses_stores tables. */
/* Q62. Store only date_of_birth as a column, and expose age exclusively via a computed/generated column or a view expression (AGE(date_of_birth)) - since age is never stored, it can never become redundantly out of sync. */
/* Q63. In a category_tax_rates(category_id PK/FK, tax_rate) table, joined from products via category_id - keeping it there (not duplicated on every product or order line) keeps tax_rate dependent only on category, satisfying 3NF. */
/* Q64. campaigns(campaign_id PK, ...), platforms(platform_id PK, platform_name), campaign_platform_spend(campaign_id FK, platform_id FK, spend) with spend attached to the specific campaign-platform combination via a composite key. */
/* Q65. Redesign into customer_phones(customer_id FK, phone_number, phone_type) with one row per phone number instead of fixed columns - this fixes the 1NF violation (repeating group crammed into separate columns). */
/* Q66. returns(return_id PK, order_id FK REFERENCES orders) plus return_items(return_id FK, order_item_id FK REFERENCES order_items) as a junction so a single return can cover multiple specific items from that order. */
/* Q67. product_suppliers(product_id FK, supplier_id FK, cost) as a junction table with a composite key (product_id, supplier_id), letting the same product have multiple supplier rows each with its own cost. */
/* Q68. Use the natural key (the PIN code string itself) as the primary key, since PIN codes are inherently stable, government-assigned, rarely-changing, and meaningful for direct lookups - a surrogate id would add an indirection with no real benefit here. */
/* Q69. order_items.order_id is declared as a NOT NULL foreign key referencing orders.order_id, so the database physically rejects any order_item row that doesn't reference a real, existing order - it can never point to the "wrong" or a missing order. */
/* Q70. It should be a derived/materialized object (a materialized view refreshed on a schedule, or computed on demand), not a hand-maintained base table, because it's fully recomputable from orders/order_items and keeping a separately-written base table in sync invites drift and duplicate logic. */
/* Q71. categories(category_id PK, category_name, parent_category_id FK REFERENCES categories(category_id) NULL) - a self-referencing foreign key where parent_category_id points to another row's category_id, forming the hierarchy. */
/* Q72. Add an is_deleted boolean (or deleted_at timestamp) column and filter it out in normal queries instead of physically deleting the row, so existing foreign keys pointing to it stay valid; the downside is every query and constraint must now remember to account for "soft-deleted but still present" rows. */
/* Q73. (warehouse_id, product_id, snapshot_date) - the combination that's guaranteed unique for exactly one inventory count per product per warehouse per day. */
/* Q74. Add a payment_methods table with a customer_id FK and an is_default boolean, then enforce with a partial UNIQUE index like UNIQUE (customer_id) WHERE is_default = true, guaranteeing at most one default row per customer at the database level. */
/* Q75. A JSON "attributes" column hides its structure from the query planner and schema (no type checking, no useful indexes on individual fields, no simple GROUP BY/aggregation), making analytics slow, error-prone, and hard to validate compared to properly typed normalized columns. */

/* Q76. sales.order_items.order_id -> sales.orders.order_id -> sales.orders.cust_id -> customers.customers.customer_id (up to the customer), and sales.order_items.prod_id -> products.products.product_id (down to the product). */
/* Q77. Periodically run a query comparing orders.net_total against SUM(order_items.net_amount) grouped by order_id, flagging any order_id where the two differ beyond a tiny rounding tolerance for investigation. */
/* Q78. Yes, arguably it should be an FK to a tiers dimension for RFM/segmentation consistency (guaranteed valid tier values, easy tier metadata lookups), though the current free-text approach is simpler and lower-overhead if tier values are few, stable, and tightly controlled by the application. */
/* Q79. Courier is modeled per-shipment in sales.shipments.courier_name (free text, not a normalized courier dimension); it's reasonably fine to denormalize as-is since it's already at the natural grain (one courier per shipment) and rarely needs cross-shipment courier metadata. */
/* Q80. It deliberately avoids storing a customer directly on returns - the customer is always reachable via the order, so there's no risk of a return's customer disagreeing with the order's actual customer; refund_amount (not return_amount) correctly names it as money given back, distinct from any original sale amount. */
/* Q81. If a store's text region is updated in one query/tool but not another (typos like "North" vs "north"), reports grouping by region text could silently split what should be one region into two different buckets. */
/* Q82. Fact table: sales.order_items (grain: one row per product per order). Dimensions: customers.customers, products.products, stores.stores, core.dim_date (via order_date). */
/* Q83. It's a reasonable simplification if RetailMart's categories are genuinely flat (no sub-categories needed for reporting); a hierarchy (parent_category_id) would be needed if the business later wants nested category rollups (e.g. "Electronics" > "Phones" > "Accessories"). */
/* Q84. There's no dedicated price-change audit table in the core schema; audit.record_changes (a generic change-log table with table_name/column_name/old_value/new_value) is the structure that could capture price changes if application code logs product price updates into it. */
/* Q85. Run a LEFT JOIN from sales.order_items to products.products ON prod_id = product_id and check for rows where products.product_id IS NULL - any such rows are orphaned references that violate referential integrity. */
/* Q86. sales.orders.net_total is a denormalized cached sum; the safeguard would be a periodic reconciliation query (or ideally a trigger) that recomputes and flags/corrects any drift against SUM(order_items.net_amount). */
/* Q87. Introduce a normalized platforms dimension table and have marketing.ads_spend and marketing.email_clicks reference platform_id instead of free text, so spend can be reliably grouped/compared even if naming conventions vary ("Google" vs "google"). */
/* Q88. Adding a payment mode only requires inserting one new row into finance.payment_modes, with no changes needed anywhere the mode is referenced by id - versus relying on every order to spell a new free-text mode identically, which risks typos and reporting fragmentation. */
/* Q89. It can be a real (if minor) redundancy risk if loyalty.members.tier_id and customers.customers.tier can disagree; ideally one should be derived from the other (or customers.tier deprecated in favor of the FK-based tier_id) to guarantee a single source of truth. */
/* Q90. customers.reviews.product_id must be declared as a NOT NULL foreign key referencing products.products.product_id, so the database rejects any review row whose product_id doesn't correspond to a real product. */
/* Q91. Join sales.orders -> customers.customers (cust_id) -> customers.addresses (customer_id) -> stores.stores (store_id) -> sales.order_items (order_id) -> products.products (prod_id) -> core.dim_brand (brand_id), pulling customer name, address, store, product, brand, quantities and totals into one wide row per order line. */
/* Q92. If orders and their line items were one table, adding an order with several products would either force null-padded extra columns or duplicate the whole order's header data per product line; splitting them lets you insert exactly one order row plus as many order_items rows as needed, with no artificial column limits. */
/* Q93. Because a page view is a fundamentally different kind of event (a web interaction, not a purchase), so giving it its own surrogate key and timestamp keeps it independently identifiable and queryable without conflating it with unrelated order semantics. */
/* Q94. The tradeoff is that without a surrogate id, any downstream table wanting to reference "this specific snapshot" must carry all three key columns, which is more verbose but arguably more meaningful/self-documenting than an opaque generated id for this naturally-keyed grain. */
/* Q95. Because a stored net_total can be stale, wrong, or manipulated, while recomputing SUM(order_items.net_amount) filtered to valid/completed order statuses always reflects the actual underlying transactions - the ground truth the CFO needs. */
/* Q96. 1:M relationships: customers.customers -> sales.orders, and products.products -> sales.order_items. An effectively M:N relationship: orders and products, resolved by the sales.order_items junction table. */
/* Q97. Both store_id and dept_id on stores.employees should be NOT NULL foreign keys referencing stores.stores.store_id and core.dim_department.dept_id respectively, guaranteeing every employee is tied to exactly one existing store and one existing department. */
/* Q98. Because those dimensions are updated rarely (a region's name or a brand's category almost never changes), the risk window where a denormalized copy could go stale is small, making the tradeoff of flattening them into reports for speed relatively low-risk. */
/* Q99. (1) Update anomalies if a customer's address or order data changes but only some flattened rows get updated. (2) Redundant storage bloating the table many times over. (3) Loss of referential integrity guarantees, since a single flat table can't enforce that every order's customer or address actually exists as expected. */
/* Q100. RetailMart keeps its OLTP-style core (customers, orders, order_items, products) properly normalized for integrity and safe concurrent writes; it deliberately denormalizes only in a few controlled places (net_total, dimension tables like dim_region/dim_brand) for read convenience; it relies on views/materialized views as the safe layer for further denormalization; and it always keeps a clear, joinable path back to normalized source-of-truth tables for reconciliation. */

/* ============================================================
   END OF ANSWER KEY - HARD LEVEL (100/100)
============================================================ */
